papers.intro:
Studying the motion of a viscous incompressible fluid past an obstacle is a classical problem in fluid mechanics. Various models for the fluid are being used, such as the Navier-Stokes, Euler or Stokes equations, holding in different physical contexts. The question when each of these models is a good description of the underlying system is both interesting mathematically and physically. In particular, viscous fluids at low viscosity are poorly understood due to turbulence and chaos phenomena. Closely connected is the vanishing viscosity limit: for a viscous fluid, typically described by the Navier-Stokes equations, how well is it described when the viscosity is extremely small, by the Euler equations, where no viscous terms are considered? While convergence of solutions to the Navier-Stokes equations to the Euler equations in the entire space is well known on small time intervals [36], in general bounded domains, the formation of boundary layers makes this question much more delicate and has hence attracted much research (see for example [26, 17] and the survey [30]). In this work, we consider the vanishing viscosity limit in the whole space around a small obstacle: the obstacle creates a localized disturbance of the velocity field, while the viscosity determines how this disturbance is transmitted into the surrounding fluid. Since the obstacle is very small, one hopes that the effect by these boundary layers near the obstacle are negligible. In [21], this question has already been considered for the homogeneous Navier-Stokes equations: indeed, convergence of Navier-Stokes to Euler holds as long as the size of the obstacle is less than a constant times the viscosity. Questions of this type occur naturally in homogenization, effective-medium theories, and the analysis of dilute suspensions, as these settings deal with the mathematical description of flows containing small obstacles, such as particles or droplets (see for example [18, 1] and references therein). Even when an individual obstacle is small compared with the characteristic length scale of the flow, its effect may accumulate or interact with singular limits. In many physical situations, the fluid is not homogeneous, but may have different density in space (such as in the atmosphere, where the density becomes much lower with altitude, or more generally various kinds of suspensions). In this case, it is more suitable to work with the inhomogeneous Navier-Stokes equations. Another example are the multiphase flows, here we can model the different phases as one inhomogeneous fluid. In the case of an incompressible fluid, the density is simply transported by the fluid. Thus, the small-scale modification of the velocity near the obstacle can in principle be transported over macroscopic distances through the density equation. In this work, we extend the results of [21] to the inhomogeneous Navier-Stokes equations. Moreover, along the way, we can show some improved convergence rates, leading to convergence of not only the fluid velocity, but also its gradient.

